Z||000012||Oreif||04-06-2001||12:36 PM||||Did you change the pick-up coil? That's what went on my car last year. It requires the distributor to be pulled out but it only takes about an hour of work to do it all.<BR>Here is our club article on how to do it step by step. <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-pickupcoil.html</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||3
Z||000013||MooCow||04-06-2001||01:05 PM||||Hey oreif what is the pick-coil??  Isnt that what I bought??  I bought a new coil along with the ingnition modual and new cap and rotor.  I think my coil is different cause on that web page I never marked anything.  I just unpluged wires and pluged it in to the new things I bought.<P>                                  Thanks Ken||||reg||5
Z||000014||Taijiguy||04-06-2001||01:25 PM||||Any codes? The ignition module will generally toss a code. <P>Did you check to see if you're getting power to the ignition module, and the coil? How did you check spark...did you pull the coil wire from the cap, or just a plug wire? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||stsmithgt||04-06-2001||02:09 PM||||I had the pick-up coil fail me twice in one year. One time it was real intermitant.  It never tossed me a code, only tossed me to the curb. HAH!  That thing seems to be an almost annual piece to replace from heat or corrosion messing it up.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||04-06-2001||02:13 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MooCow:<BR><B>Hey oreif what is the pick-coil??  Isnt that what I bought??  I bought a new coil along with the ingnition modual and new cap and rotor.  I think my coil is different cause on that web page I never marked anything.  I just unpluged wires and pluged it in to the new things I bought.<P>                                  Thanks Ken</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The pick-up coil is inside the distributor. If you take the cap off you'll see the ignition module. The 2-wire connector on the back side hooks to a ring around the shaft. The part looks like a plastic ring (almost like the plastic ring wire come on) with gold wires wound around it. There are 2-wires that come off of it. If I remember correctly they are green and white. (not sure) You'll also see a star type of ring on the inside. The star ring spins with the shaft and the points of the star cause the coil to pick-up the position of the shaft. It takes the place of the points and condenser on the older vehicles. To replace the coil you need to remove the distributor, take off the gear on the bottom and slide the shaft out of the distributor housing. These coils go out due to heat. The wires get brittle and crack. Follow the link I posted before for the instructions. <BR>The Ignition coil is the one that has the connector and the spark plug type wire going to the center of the distributor cap.<P>If you drive the car as a daily drive, many folks in our club replace them every 30,000 and keep a spare with the tools required in the trunk. It's one of the few things that will stop the car cold without setting any codes. <P><BR>Hope this helps.<P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Taijiguy||04-06-2001||02:38 PM||||Hey Oreif, <BR>Is there a way to test those before yanking them? Like, what should a normal resistance reading on one of those be, any idea?<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Oreif||04-06-2001||02:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>Hey Oreif, <BR>Is there a way to test those before yanking them? Like, what should a normal resistance reading on one of those be, any idea?<P>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-06-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My new one read about 2 ohms when new and 999,999,999,999 megohms (open) when bad. It's basically a winding of wire. I am not sure what the ohm range would be where they still work.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||thomas_l||04-06-2001||04:01 PM||||You need to back up and test from the beginning and quit throwing parts (and $$) on it. If you now have a spark, you may have just gassed the plugs from all the previous cranking! Also, nobody has mentioned seeing if there is 12V on the primary side of the coil - use a test light if you don't have a DVOM. A blown fuse or fuse-link on either the ignition circuit or ECM will take away the fire real quick.<P>The resistance varies and is difficult to use as a guide because it is very low. The only real good way to diagnose a pick-up coil is with a scope. Since it is a throw-away part (under $50) and the old one is probably marginal at best from old age and heat, and often breaks when you start pulling and poking at it with test leads, it's pretty safe to get it.<P>Wow I almost wrote an entire book on the subject! But then I think that books already available ;-D (Honest, I'm not being mean or sarcastic) Before you buy any more parts save your money go to the library and check out a chilton manual that has a troubleshooting tree on your model's ignition system. Maybe someone on this forum will scan & email you one right out of the Official Shop Manual. You might even have to borrow a test light or DVOM, but you will end up spending less money.||||reg||1
Z||000020||fyerstarter||04-06-2001||04:32 PM||||-Check for spark (pull a plug, ground it, crank it)<P>-If there is spark then check for fuel (there is a little bleeder deal up under the right side of your intake manifold plenum, its like the valve in a tire, have someone crank it and push the center part in with your fingernail, if gas shoots out you have fuel pressure)<P>-If you have fuel and spark then make absolutely sure that your spark plug wires are on correctly. You may have put them on wrong after replacing the distributor and module. I did the same thing, I had it lined up exactly like the Chiltons manual illustration but it was wrong. Took me forever to figure that one out, rotated all the plug wires one to the left on the dist cap and VROOOOOOOM started right up.||||reg||1
